# cp.pdxdoc — long-form doc for `doc cp`
#
# Wave: R50  Milestone: M5 (1.0 signed release)
# Doc format: `design/tooling/plan.md` I7 §2 in the paideia-os repo.
# Rendered by the `doc` tool (Category F, P0) — parses this file per
# `doc.M1-002 .pdxdoc file-format parser` and emits a paginated,
# cross-referenced text render to KIND_TTY.
#
# The .pdxdoc format is line-oriented: `@section`, `@subsection`,
# `@para`, `@example`, `@xref`, and `@posix-diff` directives are
# recognised by the doc parser at doc.M1-002; every other line is
# body text within the current section. Ordering is significant —
# the parser walks the file top-to-bottom to build the render tree.

@doc-format-version 1
@tool cp
@version 1.0.0
@one-liner Copy a file or a directory tree in one atomic PdxFS transaction.

@section 1. Name

@para
cp — copy a file or a directory tree, atomically, with per-file
capability re-signing and a PdxFS undo record on overwrite.

@section 2. Synopsis

@example
cp [--flag ...] [-flag ...] <src> <dst>
cp -r <src-dir> <dst-dir>

@para
Two positional arguments — source path and destination path — are
required. When `-r` is present, the source is a directory tree and
the destination is the parent directory of the target tree. When
`-r` is absent the source must be a regular file.

@section 3. Description

@para
cp copies bytes from `<src>` to `<dst>` under a single PdxFS
transaction: every write is either fully visible at the destination
or fully rolled back. Partial-copy states are not visible to any
concurrent reader; a mid-copy crash or a mid-copy signal that ends
the process leaves the destination byte-identical to its pre-copy
state.

@para
Every cp invocation also (a) journals a `CopyRecord` to the audit
log before writing any bytes (D3 audit-first — the record is
present even on failure), (b) emits `CopyProgressRecord[]` on its
stdout fd bound to schema `CopyProgressRecord@0.1` (D2 semantic
pipes — downstream `query`, `sort`, `pipeviz` consume the typed
records without column-slicing text), and (c) attempts to re-sign
the destination inode's cap tail under the invoker's `user_sk`
so ownership provenance survives the copy (`design/user/model.md`
§10.2 signed-inode field). When the invoker's key is locked or
the copy crosses user boundaries, the re-sign gracefully degrades;
the file bytes still land and `--verbose` emits a one-line
diagnostic naming the degrade cause.

@para
When `--over-existing` is present and the destination already
exists, cp writes a PdxFS v1 undo record before overwriting so
`undo cp <same-args>` restores the pre-copy destination bytes.
Without `--over-existing`, an existing destination is treated as
an error (exit 1) — this differs from POSIX cp which silently
overwrites by default.

@section 4. Options

@subsection 4.1 cp-specific flags

@example
-r                    Recursively copy a directory tree.
-v                    Emit CopyProgressRecord[] per file on stdout.
--dry-run             Preview the copy without writing to dst.
--over-existing       Write undo record before overwriting.

@subsection 4.2 Standard flag vocabulary (I3)

@example
--help                Render this document via `doc cp`.
--version             Print `cp 1.0.0 (build <hash>) sig <fingerprint>`.
--json                Emit CopyProgressRecord[] as JSON.
--schema              Print CopyProgressRecord schema and exit.
--verbose             Additional diagnostics on stderr (audit ids).
--quiet               Suppress non-error diagnostics.
--no-cap:<name>       Drop the named cap before exec (per D5).
--pdx-schema          Set emit_schema=1 in ParsedArgs.

@section 5. Exit codes

@example
0    success.
1    operation failed (open/read/write error, short write).
2    usage error (parse fail, wrong positional count).
3    recognised shape, body not yet implemented (see M-diff).
4    cap denied (elevate refused, cross-user preservation locked).

@para
Code 3 is a bootstrap-era reservation — it names an argv shape the
parser accepts but the milestone body does not yet implement. At
1.0, `-r` at pos_count > 2 is the last surviving exit-3 site;
every other flag body is wired.

@section 6. Capabilities required

@para
Every cp invocation receives these caps from the loader's InitCap
sidecar per `caps.decl`. The loader narrows the two file caps'
scope to the argument path prefixes at exec — cp cannot read or
write outside the source and destination-parent subtrees named on
argv.

@example
KIND_USER              (self)
KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT      (invoke)
KIND_PDXFS_FILE        (read,  <src>)
KIND_PDXFS_FILE        (write, <dst-parent>)
KIND_PDXFS_TXN         (invoke)

@para
Cross-subtree destinations trigger a KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL request
at M3-003. The request specifies the widened `KIND_PDXFS_FILE
(write, <dst-parent>)` cap; the approver hop returns either a
widened cap (auto-approve or human-approve OK) or a refuse. On
refuse, cp exits 4 with a stderr diagnostic naming the missing
cap. cp never opens a broker hop the substrate does not yet expose —
if the elevate broker is unreachable, cp exits 4 with a distinct
diagnostic.

@section 7. Output schemas

@para
cp binds fd 1 (stdout) to `CopyProgressRecord@0.1` at
`main.pdx::cp_main` before dispatching. Every successful per-file
copy emits one record naming the source path, destination path,
byte count copied, elapsed wall-clock, and the cap-tail preserve
outcome (OK / DEGRADED_KEY_LOCKED / DEGRADED_CROSS_USER).
Downstream:

@example
$ cp -r /src/tree /dst/tree | query 'select path where bytes > 1M'
$ cp -r /src/tree /dst/tree | pipeviz --bar bytes

@para
The record schema hash is stable across every 1.0.x release. A
schema-incompatible bump is a 2.0 concern; the M3-001 `--schema`
flag prints the current schema definition on stdout for offline
inspection.

@section 8. Audit + undo

@para
Every cp invocation writes an audit record to `/system/audit/user-
events/` before it writes any bytes. On success the record is
sealed with the outcome (bytes copied, elapsed) at commit time. On
failure the seal contains the failure code and the partial state
was rolled back at the TXN boundary — the audit log names the
attempt even when nothing landed.

@para
`--over-existing` additionally writes a PdxFS v1 undo record to the
destination's parent subtree. `undo cp <same-args>` walks the undo
graph and restores the pre-copy destination bytes. Multi-file
recursive copies write one undo record per over-written destination;
`undo` replays them in reverse order.

@section 9. POSIX differences

@posix-diff
POSIX cp overwrites existing destinations by default; paideia-os
cp refuses (exit 1) unless `--over-existing` is present.

@posix-diff
POSIX cp emits no output on success; paideia-os cp emits
CopyProgressRecord[] on stdout (empty when the source is empty).
`--quiet` suppresses the records for POSIX-shell scripts that
expected silence.

@posix-diff
POSIX cp does not journal to an audit log; paideia-os cp writes
a CopyRecord before every operation (audit-first per D3).

@posix-diff
POSIX cp treats every copy as opaque bytes; paideia-os cp attempts
cap-tail re-signing at every destination so ownership provenance
travels with the file.

@posix-diff
POSIX cp exit codes are 0 / >0; paideia-os cp distinguishes usage
(2), not-yet-implemented (3), and cap-denied (4) so scripts can
branch on the cause.

@section 10. Examples

@example
# Single file, silent success.
$ cp /home/alice/notes.md /tmp/notes.md
$ echo $?
0

@example
# Recursive tree copy with progress records piped to a filter.
$ cp -r /src/photos /backup/photos | query 'where bytes > 4M'

@example
# Overwrite an existing destination with a rollback point.
$ cp --over-existing /new/config /etc/paideia/config
$ undo cp --over-existing /new/config /etc/paideia/config
$ diff /etc/paideia/config /old/config    # matches; undo restored it.

@example
# Preview without touching the destination.
$ cp --dry-run -r /src /dst
cp: would copy 214 files (127.4 MiB) into /dst

@example
# Cross-subtree destination — elevate hop.
$ cp report.pdf /srv/shared/2026/
cp: requesting KIND_PDXFS_FILE(write, /srv/shared/2026/) via elevate...
cp: approved (auto-policy: /srv/shared/**/ writable by user alice).

@example
# Cross-user copy — signed-inode degrades, bytes still land.
$ cp -v /home/alice/report.pdf /home/bob/inbox/report.pdf
cp: cap-tail preserve DEGRADED_CROSS_USER (destination unsigned).

@section 11. Files

@example
caps.decl              Capability manifest (baseline cap set).
manifest.pdxproj       paideia-as build manifest.
manifest.pdxsig        Dual-signed release manifest (author + root).
CHANGELOG.md           Per-version change log.
doc/cp.pdxdoc          This document.
src/*.pdx              Source (12 modules, one responsibility each).
tests/*.md             Test-spec matrices (18 rows across 3 files).

